<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- Decoding picture bytes into something a window can draw - and the one project in the
       repository that is allowed to reference WPF without being an application.

       Why it exists at all: the decode sat as a private method inside App.xaml.cs, where no test
       can reach it. That is the same place, and the same reason, that once let a display fail to
       ever reconnect (Part 6, and the open decision on the connection loop in STATUS.md). The
       seam this closes is a real one: what MagickCodec WRITES has so far only ever been read back
       by Magick itself, and the JPEG it passes through is trimmed by our own byte surgery.

       Windows-bound all the way through, hence the name. WIC reaches us through PresentationCore,
       so this needs net10.0-windows AND UseWPF - there is nothing in here that would build on
       Linux, and pretending otherwise would only put it in the Linux filter to fail there.

       What deliberately does NOT live here: WHICH size to decode at. The base step is the longer
       screen edge (Part 6), and that is arithmetic over numbers - it stays platform-neutral in
       Core, and this project takes the result as a parameter. The split is what keeps the
       untestable part as small as it can be. -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <UseWPF>true</UseWPF>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
